diff --git a/crit-loop/round-4/crit.md b/crit-loop/round-4/crit.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..5869af7
--- /dev/null
+++ b/crit-loop/round-4/crit.md
@@ -0,0 +1,25 @@
+**First read.** The eye lands on the headline, which now stacks in three short lines on the left. It moves right to the tide card, which now fills two thirds of the width, with its bold times in a clear middle column, and then back to the dark "Set up your harbor" button. The page reads as a plain, sober utility for harbor offices. It looks believable and calm, and the tide card now looks like the page's main evidence, not a sidebar. On the tablet and phone the page is one column: headline, button, card, then three notes. On the tablet the column now runs to the nav's right edge, and on widescreen the layout is the desktop layout, centered.
+
+**What it's for.** It gets a harbor master to set up Tidewell, so that berth holders can check tides, lock gate times and pontoon depth on one page (taken from the work order's crit).
+
+**Verdict.** The layout fixes worked. The card is on the grid and has room, and the tablet column now spans the full width. But the two content findings are untouched. The page still asks the office to "stand behind" the tide data without saying where the data comes from or who works out the gate times.
+
+**Finding status**
+- headline-trust-unbacked: open. At all four viewports, "Gate openings are worked out from each tide" still has no subject, and no line says where the tide data comes from or what the office checks.
+- table-does-not-say-what-it-is: open. At all four viewports, "Today at Porthmellin" still goes straight into the table, and no line says it is a sample of what berth holders see.
+- tide-card-cramped: closed. The card's left edge is at x 491 on desktop and x 811 on widescreen, the same as the second feature column. Per the facts file, the Time column is now 155 px wide (was 67 px) and the Height column 147 px (was 63 px).
+- tablet-content-short-of-nav: closed. On the tablet, the h1 and the hero paragraph now run from x 64 to x 704 (640 px), so their right edge matches the right edge of "Sign in" (x 704). The card and the notes also end there.
+
+**New problems.** None.
+
+**What would move it most**
+1. The trust claim has nothing behind it (the headline and the "Lock gate times" note, all viewports). A harbor master reads "can stand behind" and then "Gate openings are worked out" with nobody named as doing the work. They cannot tell whether this is a source they can vouch for or a black box. Source: Nielsen, match between the system and the real world, and plain-language guidance against a passive with no agent. Fix direction: give "worked out" a subject, and add one line near the headline or the note that says where the tide data comes from and what the office checks or approves. Only the maker has these facts, so the loop cannot close this finding until the maker supplies them. Defect. Viewports: desktop, phone, tablet, widescreen.
+2. The tide card does not say what it is (the "Today at Porthmellin" heading, all viewports). Now that the card is the biggest thing in the hero, a visitor is even more likely to read it as live tides for a real harbor, not as a sample of the page their berth holders will get. Fix direction: add one short line under the heading that says this is a sample of what berth holders see. Convention. Viewports: desktop, phone, tablet, widescreen.
+
+**Smaller things.**
+- Desktop and widescreen: the narrower hero column breaks the headline into three lines of about 16 characters ("Tide times your / harbor office can / stand behind"). It reads a little choppy, but the top of the headline (y 152) and the top of the card (y 145) now line up well. Leave it unless the headline copy changes. Viewports: desktop, widescreen.
+
+**What's working.**
+- The tide card on the feature grid: its left edge now shares x 491 with "Depth at the pontoon", so the hero and the notes below read as one structure.
+- The table itself: the times are bold and the heights are right-aligned, so the numbers a berth holder needs come first.
+- The restraint: one accent color, on the button only, and no added decoration. That fits a tool that wants to be trusted.
